From cc674ff533f2bf5b63e907e1cc764f33f77b5cea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bringup <bringup@t1100.local>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:59:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 15/16] soc: mediatek: mtk-ramconsole: write persistent_ram
 directly, no console

The board still dies before the ramoops console exists, so on the last two
boot images /proc/last_kmsg showed nothing at all: not a partial log, not an
"mrcm:" line - the zone still held an older boot's log. Two independent facts
explain it.

1. The zone is system RAM (lk reserves it and it stays inside the memory
   map), and arm64 refuses to ioremap RAM:
     __ioremap_prot(): "Don't allow RAM to be mapped" -> WARN -> NULL.
   So mrcm_locate() got NULL from ioremap(), mrcm_base stayed NULL and the
   driver returned -ENODEV after printing into a ring buffer nobody can read.
   No log, no crash, nothing.

2. Even with a mapping, the previous version only wrote after
   register_console(CON_PRINTBUFFER) + pr_info() + unregister_console(),
   which is ramoops' own mechanism - if printk does not replay into the
   console for whatever reason, the zone stays untouched.

Rewrite it to do the one thing that cannot be skipped: build the
persistent_ram console record by hand (12-byte header "DBGC"/start/size plus
the text ring, exactly fs/pstore/ram_core.c's struct
persistent_ram_buffer), so ramoops later finds a valid "existing buffer" and
appends to it instead of zapping it.

Zone location no longer touches the device tree: values come from
ramoops.mem_address/mem_size/console_size/pmsg_size/ftrace_size on
boot_command_line (and must not be read from DT - lk spins if a static
reserved-memory node overlaps its own reservations), with hardcoded
fallbacks 0x54410000/0xe0000/0x40000 matching the stock cmdline.

Mapping: memremap(MEMREMAP_WB) (returns the linear mapping for valid RAM),
falling back to ioremap() for a non-RAM region. Text written through the
linear mapping sits in the caches, so each snapshot ends with
dcache_clean_poc(): if the SoC dies and the board is reset, DRAM must already
contain the log.

Snapshots: early_initcall (the earliest initcall there is - before SMP and
before any driver), postcore_initcall_sync (around ramoops registration, also
restores the ring if ramoops zapped it), arch_initcall and
late_initcall_sync. Each one rewrites the ring from the current printk buffer
via kmsg_dump_rewind()/kmsg_dump_get_line() (chronological, no console
registration involved) and starts with a "T1100LOG mrcm: stage=..." line, so
the presence of that line alone proves the black box ran.

Bring-up aid only.
---
 drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-ramconsole.c | 419 +++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 246 insertions(+), 173 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-ramconsole.c b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-ramconsole.c
index 60f6982d6..44cf63c92 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-ramconsole.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-ramconsole.c
@@ -8,222 +8,274 @@
  * recovery exposes /proc/last_kmsg, and its contents come from the ramoops
  * console zone in DRAM, which survives a warm reset.
  *
- * The kernel already has a working path into that zone: ramoops itself
- * (CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE) registers a console and, because the console is
- * registered with CON_PRINTBUFFER, replays the whole printk ring buffer -
- * that is why the log of a successful boot starts at "[0.000000] Linux
- * version". The problem is that ramoops only comes up at postcore_initcall
- * time; everything before that (setup_arch, early_initcall, core_initcall,
- * and any bus hang caused by a driver) is invisible.
+ * ramoops itself cannot help before postcore_initcall time: it registers a
+ * console there and only then starts writing. Everything before that is
+ * invisible, and so is any hang that happens while the kernel is still
+ * bringing up the PMIC/DRAM rails.
  *
- * This driver closes that window without ever competing with ramoops:
+ * This driver therefore writes the persistent_ram console record directly:
+ * 12-byte header (sig "DBGC", start, size) followed by a text ring, exactly
+ * the layout fs/pstore/ram_core.c expects, so ramoops later picks the buffer
+ * up as "existing buffer" and keeps appending to it instead of zapping it.
+ * No console is registered: register_console()+CON_PRINTBUFFER is the path
+ * ramoops already takes, it was tried here first, and it produced nothing on
+ * this board.
  *
- *   1. it stops the TOPRGU watchdog as the very first thing, so a hang stays
- *      a hang instead of a silent reset loop;
- *   2. at early_initcall and at postcore_initcall_sync it takes a *snapshot*
- *      of the printk ring buffer into the same console zone ramoops will use:
- *      register a console with CON_PRINTBUFFER, let printk replay the ring,
- *      unregister it again. No second writer, no bookkeeping to keep in sync
- *      with persistent_ram, and after the second snapshot ramoops takes over
- *      and appends to the same ring.
+ * Two details that cost a boot image each to learn:
  *
- * Zone discovery, in order:
- *   a) a "ramoops" device tree node, if one is ever added (mainline layout:
- *      console zone starts after the record area);
- *   b) the reserved-memory children the bootloader creates itself
- *      (mblock-N-pstore, mblock-N-ram_console, ...). They cannot be declared
- *      in our DTS - MTK LK spins forever if a static region overlaps one of
- *      its own reservations (docs/lk-dtb-rules.md) - but they *are* present
- *      in the device tree LK hands over, so they can be re-used;
- *   c) the ramoops.* kernel parameters, which is how this board configures
- *      ramoops ("ramoops: using module parameters" in the log).
+ *   - The zone lives in *system RAM* (the bootloader reserves it and it stays
+ *     in the memory map), and arm64 refuses to ioremap RAM:
+ *     __ioremap_prot() -> "Don't allow RAM to be mapped" -> NULL. The first
+ *     version of this driver ioremapped the zone, got NULL, printed
+ *     "no ramoops console zone found" into a ring buffer nobody could read,
+ *     and did nothing at all. Use memremap(MEMREMAP_WB), which for valid RAM
+ *     returns the linear mapping.
+ *   - Text written through that mapping sits in the caches. If the SoC dies
+ *     and the board is reset, DRAM only has what was cleaned to the point of
+ *     coherency, so every snapshot ends with dcache_clean_poc().
  *
- * The first version of this driver only looked for (a) and therefore did
- * nothing at all on this board: of_find_compatible_node(NULL, NULL, "ramoops")
- * returned NULL, nzones stayed 0 and the driver bailed out with -ENODEV.
+ * The zone geometry comes from the ramoops.* kernel parameters only (the
+ * board passes them on the command line; there is no ramoops DT node and
+ * declaring one is not an option - MTK lk spins forever if a static
+ * reserved-memory region overlaps its own reservations). If the parameters
+ * are missing, hardcoded fallbacks matching refs/device/cmdline.txt are used,
+ * so the log works even with a truncated command line.
+ *
+ * Snapshots are taken at early_initcall (before SMP and before any driver),
+ * postcore_initcall_sync (around the time ramoops registers), arch_initcall
+ * and late_initcall_sync. Each one rewrites the ring from scratch with the
+ * current printk buffer, so the last completed snapshot is the one that
+ * survives. "T1100LOG" marks the start of a snapshot, which makes it obvious
+ * whether the black box itself ran at all.
  *
  * Bring-up aid only; remove once a real console exists.
  */
 
